From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tiberiu Chibici <chibicitiberiu@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 03:26:21 +0200
Subject: nouveau, nv30: out of GART, a context keeps its fence and texture
 uploads go through the CPU

SuperTuxKart crashed on the PS3 (2026-10-26) after hundreds of "ps3rsx: out of GART": a segfault
at 0x20 in _nouveau_fence_next(), nv->fence->state with nv->fence NULL, from
nv30_context_kick_notify() (a texture upload's util_throttle_memory_usage() waiting on a fence).
An earlier kick had dropped the context's fence and failed to allocate the next one: every fence
has a 4 KiB GART buffer to wait on, and the GART had none. kick_notify() returning false also made
pushbuf_submit() drop that whole batch. And with the GART full, texture uploads were lost: no
staging buffer, nv30_miptree_transfer_map() returned NULL (GL_OUT_OF_MEMORY, which games don't
check) and the texture kept old pixels.

- nouveau_fence: _nouveau_fence_next() makes the next fence before it lets the current one go, so
  a context always has a current fence. A fence whose wait object cannot be had waits for the
  GPU's submitted work first (nouveau_fence_reclaim(): signalled fences free their wait objects,
  their works free staging buffers and storage) and tries again; failing that it goes without
  one: it is emitted without the buffer reference, and waiting on it polls the sequence (10 s).
  Only a failed malloc of the fence itself fails, and then the old fence stays current.
- _nouveau_fence_update(flushed) marks what was emitted as submitted even when the GPU's sequence
  did not move (it returned early before), so the reclaim can tell submitted fences from those of
  the batch being built, which it must not wait on (that would submit from kick_notify).
- nouveau_fence_wait_idle(): waits for the context's current fence with a reference held; the
  wait submits and replaces it, and nouveau_fence_wait(nv->fence) could read it after it was
  deleted (nv30_staging_bo_new() did).
- nv30_staging_bo_new(): a staging buffer that cannot be had waits for the GPU, gives the staging
  pools' idle buffers back to the kernel, and tries again (before: only with uploads in flight).
- nv30_miptree_transfer_map(): still none: the data goes through malloc memory, and the CPU copies
  it into the texture at the unmap (or out of it at the map, for reads). On the PS3 textures are
  CPU-mapped VRAM (nv30_miptree_create() asks for it); mapping for the write waits for the GPU's
  use of the texture. Swizzled, linear and compressed layouts, as nv30_transfer_rect_cpu() does
  them (nv30_transfer_rect_cpu_map(), now with whole rows for linear copies). Multisampled
  textures and buffers the CPU cannot map still fail the map, cleanly.
- nouveau_transfer_staging() (buffer maps): no GART for the staging area waits for the GPU and
  tries again; a staging buffer whose map fails is freed (it leaked).
- nv30_fragprog_upload(): no buffer or no map for a fragment program (out of memory, after the
  stream copy failed) keeps the bound program and uploads this one at its next bind; it wrote
  through a NULL map. The blit's fragment program the same way: no blit instead of fp->bo of NULL.
- nv30_transfer_rect_cpu(): a buffer the CPU cannot map loses the copy instead of the process.

Host tests: mesa/tests/alloc-fail-test.c (fences with a full and with no GART, against a fake GPU)
and mesa/tests/transfer-cpu-test.c (texture transfers with a full GART), both in tools/run-tests.sh
--only mesa-query-steal.

